I was recently with John Lewis crossing  the Edmund Pettus Bridge in Selma and  recalling dr. King's leadership John  Lewis's leadership so many who in their  20s had the courage to speak about the  ideals of our country in an environment  where they were exposed to physical harm  to intimidation to hate and ultimately  for dr. King death and fifty years of  reflection on what his life meant is to  reflect on one of the greatest leaders  of our country it is to think about  someone who had the vision to know what  we can be even if we hadn't achieved it  at the point when he lived he was  aspirational but he was also very  realist he understood that those words  we spoke at 1776 have meaning we are all  and should be treated as equals he had  the the knowledge and I think deep faith  to believe that we would hold true to  the ideals that were behind the  principles that were the founding of our  country that the Constitution of the  United States and you know and I've been  reading his writings recently it's just  they gave me inspiration and there's a  whole series of speeches that he gave  about the need for justice and equality  that was about race and and it's gender  and ethnicity and also about economic  justice and when we look at where we are  in this country at this moment in time  we know that those are still issues that  we need to fight for including economic  justice interestingly enough also he he  died 50 years ago so during his life he  was also writing about the importance of  understanding the impact of technology  but he talked about computers and what  we needed to do as the world was  changing and things were being  computerized to think about how  we will achieve equality for everyone in  that new world and in particular workers  and working people and look at where we  are today where we're having these  conversations what will this mean  technology to the workforce what will it  mean in terms of are we going to have an  equal shot everyone at the American  dream will we have equal level of skills  and parity in terms of our value as  working people so those are all the  things I think about when I think about  him I think about the life he lived  which was a life of deep faith and  conviction and belief in our country and  the best of who we are and also a vision  for understanding where we can't be in  and where we might be in the future  nothing is new Under the Sun nothing is  new Under the Sun and he understood that  there are certain values that will be  constant such as love thy neighbor you  know treat each person with dignity and  respect  our value to have a society and a civil  society and and that means a society  that values the lack of violence which  is also about safety and making sure  that we guarantee everyone's safety and  their ability to speak and free speech  and freedom of association and those are  evergreen concepts those are concepts  that are about our values and hopefully  our values stay constant even when  circumstances and the environment may  change the tragedy about his life cut so  short is is the tragedy about people  attempting to kill his voice and but his  voice is and was so powerful that his  voice still we hear it right and in  particular what was tragic is that his  his work was just on the verge of really  pushing for the economic justice piece  before he was killed he remember it was  the sanitation workers he was he was  there to stand for their right to  collective bargain for their right to  have a workplace  would be safe there right to have  dignified wages and and to have the  ability to have a retirement that give  them the ability dignity that's what his  fight was before he was killed and that  is a fight that remains true today you  can go across this country and meet with  hardworking Americans who very much want  to know that if they work hard and they  play by the rules that they will have a  shot at the American Dream that they  will have a shot at homeownership one of  the big issues for students in this  country that they will have a shot at  getting a meaningful education and not  being saddled with student debt these  are all economic issues and these are  all issues that are about economic  justice and dr. King understood that you  can't have equality among people without  everyone having an equal shot at  economic freedom and dignity in that way  you know my parents met when they were  active in the civil rights movement and  so I feel like dr. King and so many of  the leaders of those days were present  in my mind you know at the moment of my  birth and but certainly there have been  different phases in my life that his his  words and his courage and his leadership  have been poignant and including just  recently being in Selma dr. King has  inspired me in so many ways and mostly  it is that he he shows that you can have  the courage to speak certain truths and  even though they may invite a difficult  conversation people will respect those  conversations and and he also most  importantly I think especially at this  moment in time made very clear that we  all have so much more in common than  what separates us and that is such an  important statement that every leader  should make but first they should feel  that and then say it and say it say it  loudly we hope we all as Americans have  so much more in common than what  separates us and we can never succumb to  forces that are about hate or division  we are stronger when we understand that  we are one unified people and and we are  better we are better for it and he  understood that and that inspires me  every day I think that he was one of the  figures of the those days when we were  fighting so much who understood the  power of expression and to to actually  feel that what the people were feeling  and then put those feelings into words I  think that his power was also about his  ability to coalition build again back to  that point about seeing that we have so  much more in common than what separates  us in the midst of being seemingly so  different so he brought together you  know he brought together preachers and  rabbis he brought together people of  every walk of life from every age and  race and every geographic region of the  country he understood that that if he  was going to have that fight on what we  need to do around civil rights but also  economic justice he brought together  organized labor together with the folks  that were you know active in the civil  rights movement understanding that their  their struggles and fight were won and  born out of one one desire which is to  again to have equality I think that's  part of the power that he had he  understood bring people together and and  and you can actually you know succeed I  think it's critically important that we  understand that that these fights these  fights for for fairness for freedom for  equality for justice these are the this  is a this is a fight for something and  not against something and let's be  inspired by that it and and mostly what  he stood for is understanding that this  is about fighting for the best of who we  are as a country  and that fight is born out of love of  country this is a fight that is born out  of love of country and wanting us to be  the best of who we are and highlighting  the best of who we are and fighting for  the ideals that are the basis of who we  are and what makes us Americans if you  know one of my favorite quotes but I'm  gonna tell you it's actually he also  made a really wonderful decision to  marry a woman by the name of Creta Scott  King and actually it's I'm gonna it so  I'm gonna attribute that this is her  quote and I'll paraphrase but she said  the the fight for civil rights the fight  for equality and fight for justice must  be fought and won with each generation  and when she said that I think she  understood he understood that this is  something that is whatever gains we make  they're not going to be permanent that's  the nature of it so let's not lament  let's not be overwhelmed let's not throw  up our hands when it's time to roll up  our sleeves the very nature of it that  we must each and every day fight for the  ideals of our country I think that we  have made great strides and we have a  lot more to do I think that the way that  I think of it is we are I don't I don't  think of it as being a pendulum where we  swing back and forth I don't think it's  that binary I think we we are constantly  striving to do better but it's like a  loop and and sometimes we dip a little  and then we go a little better and then  we dip and then we then we get better  but ultimately we're on an upward  trajectory but I'm an optimist and a  realist I see what's wrong I mean I see  right now that there are certainly  forces of that are sowing hate and  division in our country and and we've  got to you know we have to deal with  that and we have to reject it and we  have to reject certain certain notions  you know people are saying we're a  divided country I don't believe that I  reject that I reject that notion I don't  believe at our core that we're divided I  believe we have so much more in common  what separates us and I and I know that  I know that when people wake up in the  middle of the night with that thought  that is worrying them and weighing on  them they're thinking the same thing and  it is not through the lens of the party  with which they're registered to vote it  has to do with you know their health or  their children or their parents or can  they get a job keep a job pay the bills  the vast majority of us have so much  more in common than what separates us my  dream is that we live to achieve dr.  Kings purpose and that includes equality  that is both about racial justice than  it is about economic justice it is about  all people being treated equally under  the law and in society one of the most  important things that we can do at this  moment in time is remember leaders like  dr. King who who asked us to look in a  mirror and and and think about who we  are as a country and I believe that when  we look in the mirror we will agree that  we are a great country flawed though we  may be but we have to fight for the best  of who we are and that's that's what dr.  King I think wanted us to do which is  ask each of us are we really fighting  for the best of who we are you  challenged us to that way I think
 

